# Fixture simplification: sshd contains every active Debian Linux directive;
# standalone comments and blank lines are omitted. @IF_KEYINIT@ is expanded
# exactly as debian/rules does for Linux. Ubuntu differs in its locale pam_env
# directive: user_readenv=1 precedes envfile=/etc/default/locale. The test
# exercises that variant without duplicating an otherwise identical fixture.
# common-auth/account/password are comment-free Unix-only expansions of
# the Ubuntu pam-auth-update templates plus pam-configs/unix Auth-Initial,
# Account-Initial and Password-Initial, resolving success=end to success=1.
# No optional additional package profiles are selected. These are explicit
# template expansions, not files captured from a running distribution.
# common-session is a minimal exact directive excerpt (pam_umask from the
# template, pam_unix from the Unix profile); generated primary boilerplate
# and optional additional package profiles are omitted. Tests audit include
# types and retention, not a complete running host's session policy.

Platform caveats:
Both inspected packages use ssh.service (alias sshd.service), /usr/sbin/sshd,
and an early Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf directive. Existing local
sshd_config overrides still require effective-configuration validation.
Debian installs ssh.socket without enabling it by default. Noble defaults
to socket activation: ssh.socket has Accept=no and activates ssh.service;
its generator reads configured ports/addresses. Authentication-only changes
do not require modifying listening sockets. An inactive ssh.service with
an active ssh.socket is not an absent SSH installation.
No uninspected derivatives are claimed through ID_LIKE. Non-systemd service
managers were not inspected. Debian's PAM template retains SELinux session
rules even when the host does not have enforcing SELinux enabled.
